=: title rfc-12 build service
=: date 2026-01-24
=: draft true
=: tags rfc

This post outlines the technical details for implementing our ci build system.

# vm coordinator

This go binary will listen on a pipe topic `/pico/build`
It will receive an event payload that provides the git repo or dockerfile to build and other necessary metadata
It will check if the user has a running vm
	If no vm then it'll boot up one for the user
It will send a pipe topic using the user's account `/{user}/build/{platform}`
	Platform will mimic docker's platform identifier
	The event payload will include the image and any other necessary metadata
	This topic will operate as round-robin
It listens to a pipe topic `/pico/build/inactive`
	This will be an event sent from the user coordinator to indicate the user vm is inactive
It listens to a pipe topic `/pico/build/status`
	This records the status of a build job
We want a job status table so we can present it to the user
We might want this to have a queue table so we can make some guarantees like at-least-once delivery

# user coordinator

Inside the user VM there will be a running image
The image runs a go binary
It listens for `/{user}/build/{platform}`
It runs the image based on the event payload
It runs a cron that shuts down images after a 3 hour runtime
It runs a cron that checks for inactivity
	If no user containers running after 20min then it sends a shutdown event `/pico/build/inactive` to the vm coordinator
If the payload is for a git repo
	Create a new workspace directory
	Pull the git repo into the workspace
	Run pico-manifest.sh
	Send build status to `/pico/build/status`
If the payload is a Dockfile
	Create a new workspace directory
	Create the Dockerfile
	Run it
	Send build status to `/pico/build/status`

# manifest

Shell script `pico-manifest.sh`
Shell script runs all the necessary commands for the build
Shell script will receive the payload as args
	Event type?
	Platform?
The shell script can run docker commands directly or run compose files
Every ci compose file we require a pico-status:latest image that depends on all the ci jobs in the services list.
The pico-status:latest image needs the docker socket
The image aggregates the exist codes for each job then determines success of failure
